# Summary list

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A summary list shows labelled values: a label on one side and its answer on the other, one fact to a row. Use it for the details of one device, or to review settings before saving. Several summaries on one page each need their own heading, so people can tell them apart.

Home Assistant has no card or element of this kind. A summary list is plain HTML on this site, built from the description list that [Description list](description-list.html) owns. This page owns the Civica pattern: the rows, the Change link and the rules for writing them.

This page covers:

- Summary list
  - Civica Summary List Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A summary list is a description list. Each row holds a key (the label), a value (the answer) and, if people can change it, an action.

- The key is a `dt` and the value is a `dd`.
- The action is a second `dd` holding a link, with the label “Change”.
- Each row sits in a `div`, which the description list allows.
- Long values wrap, and at 320 CSS pixels the key, value and action stack in reading order.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-summary-list-pattern"><code>Civica Summary List Pattern</code></h3>

The pattern has two forms: read-only details, and a review with a Change link on each row. A row with no way to change it has no action.

Write each row like this:

- Keep the label plain and the same each time. Say “Room”, not “Location of installation”.
- Put one fact in a value. If a value has several parts, give each its own row.
- Never leave a value blank. Write “Not supplied” when nobody gave a value and “Unavailable” when the device cannot report one.
- Make the Change link say what it changes. Add visually hidden text to the link, so that a screen reader reads “Change dashboard name” while people see “Change”. Keep each link its own target, never the whole row.
- When people return from changing a value, keep the existing answers and the way back to the review.

**Where Home Assistant lets you write it.**

- **Markdown card.** Only plain `dl`, `dt` and `dd` survive, in the browser’s default style. Classes and styles are removed. The hidden-text trick needs a class, so it cannot be used. Write the whole link text in view, such as “Change dashboard name”. See [Markdown card](markdown-card.html) for what the card keeps.
- **Entities card.** When each row is an entity with a live state, use the [Entities card and entity rows](entities-card-and-entity-rows.html) page. Home Assistant draws the rows, and the theme’s text colours apply to them.
- **Row dividers.** Changing the dividers between rows needs the card-mod integration. Test the result in your own dashboard.
- **Settings forms.** Home Assistant’s own settings forms use a settings row that has a heading and a description. A dashboard author cannot place it.

Colours come from the theme: primary and secondary text, the divider colour and the card background. Links use the theme’s primary colour, which differs between the eight themes.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A description list tells assistive technology that each value belongs to its label.
- One fact to a row keeps the list quick to scan, and long values wrap rather than being cut off.
- Screen readers announce description lists in different ways, so do not rely on the list being announced.
- A summary list shows values but cannot compare them across columns. Use a [Data table](table.html) for that.
- In a Markdown card the list has no Civica styling and no hidden link text.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a summary list for the details of one thing, or to review answers before saving. Do not use it for rows that need comparing across shared columns, for navigation or for a sequence of tasks. Use a table, a list or a task list for those.

Say what “Change” will change. A device summary should separate the friendly name, the room and the connection state, so that people never read a name as a state.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence: the key comes before its value, and rows keep their order when they stack.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text and 1.4.10 Reflow: long values wrap at 320 CSS pixels and at 200% text.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context) and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: each Change link names what it changes.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): each Change link is its own target of at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry: do not ask again for a value shown in the summary.

Plain labels, one fact to a row and a clear Change link keep the effort of reviewing low. Test an absent value, a very long device name, repeated Change links and the reading order at 320 CSS pixels. Follow the [Civica accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html) in light and dark modes.

